---
// Worked example in the step-by-step format: the same order the guides teach.
import Context from '../../../layouts/Context.astro';
import { url } from '../../../lib/url';
import ExampleStyles from '../../../components/ExampleStyles.astro';
const w = (id: string) => url(`w/${id}/`);
const g = (id: string) => url(`glossary/#${id}`);
const sources = [
  { cite: 'Adapted from Thomas Hemphill, "New Testament Textual Criticism Examples", example 2: James 2:20. Used with permission.', checked: true },
  { cite: 'United Bible Societies, The Greek New Testament, 5th ed., apparatus at James 2:20 (as reproduced in the Hemphill material above).', checked: true },
  { cite: 'Bruce M. Metzger, A Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament, 2nd ed., on James 2:20 (as quoted in the Hemphill material).', checked: true },
  { cite: 'Roger L. Omanson, A Textual Guide to the Greek New Testament, on James 2:20 (as quoted in the Hemphill material).', checked: true },
  { cite: 'NET Bible, textual note on James 2:20 (as quoted in the Hemphill material).', checked: true },
];
---
<Context title="James 2:20: useless or dead?" label="Guide" lastChecked="2026-11-20" sources={sources}
  description="A worked example where good early manuscripts disagree and the context decides.">
  <div class="case">
  <p class="lead">Some Bibles say faith without works is "useless". Others, the King James Version among them, say it is "dead". Here we work through the evidence in the same order the <a href={url('guide/weighing-evidence/')}>weighing guide</a> teaches.</p>

  <ol class="route" aria-label="The steps">
    <li><a href="#step-1-find-the-problem">Find the problem</a></li>
    <li><a href="#step-2-list-the-readings">List the readings</a></li>
    <li><a href="#step-3-external-evidence">External evidence</a></li>
    <li><a href="#step-4-internal-evidence">Internal evidence</a></li>
    <li><a href="#step-5-weigh-it-up">Weigh it up</a></li>
    <li><a href="#step-6-decide-for-your-translation">Decide</a></li>
  </ol>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 1</span>
    <h2 id="step-1-find-the-problem">Find the problem</h2>
    <p class="ask">What exactly differs between the manuscripts?</p>
    <div class="banner">
      <span class="ref">James 2:20 · UBS5 text</span>
      <p class="greek" lang="grc">θέλεις δὲ γνῶναι, ὦ ἄνθρωπε κενέ, ὅτι ἡ πίστις χωρὶς τῶν ἔργων <mark>ἀργή</mark> ἐστιν;</p>
      <p class="gloss">"Do you want to know, you empty person, that faith without works is <strong>useless</strong>?"</p>
    </div>
    <p>One word: the adjective at the end. Is faith without works <span lang="grc">ἀργή</span> (useless, idle) or <span lang="grc">νεκρά</span> (dead)?</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 2</span>
    <h2 id="step-2-list-the-readings">List the readings</h2>
    <p class="ask">Which readings are there, and who supports each one?</p>
    <ul class="readings">
      <li class="reading printed">
        <span class="tag">UBS5 text</span>
        <p class="form" lang="grc">ἀργή</p>
        <p class="meaning">"useless"</p>
        <p class="wits"><a href={w('vaticanus')}>B</a>, C* (the first hand of Codex Ephraemi), 1739 and some other minuscules, some Latin, Sahidic Coptic, Augustine</p>
      </li>
      <li class="reading">
        <p class="form" lang="grc">νεκρά</p>
        <p class="meaning">"dead"</p>
        <p class="wits"><a href={w('sinaiticus')}><span class="siglum" lang="he" dir="ltr">א</span></a>, <a href={w('alexandrinus')}>A</a>, C² (a later corrector of C), Ψ, many minuscules, <a href={w('byz')}>Byz</a>, Lect, Syriac, Bohairic Coptic</p>
      </li>
      <li class="reading">
        <p class="form" lang="grc">κενή</p>
        <p class="meaning">"empty"</p>
        <p class="wits"><a href={w('p74')}>𝔓⁷⁴</a> and one Old Latin manuscript</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href={g('star')}>*</a> marks the first hand of a manuscript, and the raised numbers its <a href={g('correctors')}>correctors</a>. The Textus Receptus reads "dead", and so does the KJV.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 3</span>
    <h2 id="step-3-external-evidence">External evidence</h2>
    <p class="ask">What do the witnesses themselves tell us? Take the six questions in order; number comes last.</p>
    <table class="qtable">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">"Useless"</th><th scope="col">"Dead"</th><th scope="col">Leans</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">How old?</th><td data-h="Useless">B, 4th century</td><td data-h="Dead"><span class="siglum" lang="he" dir="ltr">א</span>, 4th century; A, 5th</td><td><span class="lean even">even</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">From where?</th><td data-h="Useless">Greek, Latin and Sahidic (Egypt)</td><td data-h="Dead">Greek, Syriac and Bohairic (Egypt)</td><td><span class="lean even">even</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">How careful?</th><td data-h="Useless">B and 1739 are carefully copied</td><td data-h="Dead"><span class="siglum" lang="he" dir="ltr">א</span> and A are carefully copied</td><td><span class="lean even">even</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">How widespread?</th><td data-h="Useless">Several regions</td><td data-h="Dead">Several regions</td><td><span class="lean even">even</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Independent?</th><td data-h="Useless">C was first written with it, then corrected away from it</td><td data-h="Dead">The later Byzantine copies count as one line</td><td><span class="lean even">even</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">How many?</th><td data-h="Useless">Fewer</td><td data-h="Dead">Many more</td><td><span class="lean b">"dead"</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><span lang="grc">κενή</span> has only one late Greek witness and one Latin one, so we can set it aside.</p>
    <p class="sofar">The witnesses are strong on both sides: each has great fourth- and fifth-century codices. Only the count favours "dead", and the count comes last. The external evidence cannot decide.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 4</span>
    <h2 id="step-4-internal-evidence">Internal evidence</h2>
    <p class="ask">Which reading best explains how the others arose? And which would James have written?</p>
    <div class="weigh">
      <section class="side">
        <h3>For "useless"</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>What a scribe would do:</strong> verses 17 and 26 call faith without works "dead". A copyist who remembered them could easily write "dead" here too (<a href={url('guide/weighing-evidence/#internal-evidence-the-words')}>harmonization</a>). It is hard to see why anyone would change "dead" to "useless" here and leave the other two alone.</li>
          <li><strong>What James would write:</strong> <span lang="grc">ἔργων ἀργή</span> (works … useless) is a play on words, since <span lang="grc">ἀργή</span> can be read as "without work".</li>
          <li>C was first written with "useless" and later corrected to "dead": inside one manuscript, the change ran that way.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">Metzger, Omanson, the NET notes and several commentaries give these reasons.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="side">
        <h3>For "dead"</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The same arguments can be turned around: James may have written "dead" here to match verses 17 and 26.</li>
          <li>If James liked the word play, why didn't he use it in those verses too?</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small muted">Hemphill points this out.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <p><span lang="grc">κενή</span> ("empty") was probably suggested by <span lang="grc">κενέ</span> ("empty person") earlier in the same verse.</p>
    <p class="sofar">Most of the internal evidence favours "useless": it explains how "dead" arose, and the reverse is harder to explain.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 5</span>
    <h2 id="step-5-weigh-it-up">Weigh it up</h2>
    <p class="ask">Putting the steps together, which reading came first, and how sure can we be?</p>
    <ul class="scale">
      <li><b>External evidence</b><span class="lean even">even</span><span>Strong early witnesses on both sides.</span></li>
      <li><b>What a scribe would do</b><span class="lean a">"useless"</span><span>Harmonizing to verses 17 and 26 explains "dead".</span></li>
      <li><b>What James would write</b><span class="lean a">"useless"</span><span>The word play fits; the counter-argument is weaker.</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="verdict">
      <a class="badge" href={g('rating')} aria-label="Rating B: what the letters mean">{'{B}'}</a>
      <div>
        <p>UBS5 prints <span lang="grc">ἀργή</span> with a {'{B}'} rating: the committee was fairly confident.</p>
        <p>Omanson adds that here the two words differ little in meaning.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <span class="n">Step 6</span>
    <h2 id="step-6-decide-for-your-translation">Decide for your translation</h2>
    <p class="ask">What will you print, and what will you tell your readers?</p>
    <aside class="translator">
      <h3>For the translator</h3>
      <p>Follow the UBS text: "useless" or a similar word. If your church knows "dead" from an older Bible, a short footnote can say that many manuscripts read "dead". The teaching of the passage stays the same, because verses 17 and 26 say "dead" in all the main witnesses.</p>
      <p>Translator's helps do not always mark this variant. When Bibles in a language of wider communication disagree, check the apparatus yourself.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  </div>
  <ExampleStyles />
</Context>
